Q1. Deadlock Debugging (Code Trace)
Answer
Pseudo Code
// Process P1
lock(R1);
wait(100 ms);
lock(R2);
critical section;
unlock(R2);
unlock(R1);

// Process P2
lock(R2);
wait(100 ms);
lock(R1);
critical section;
unlock(R1);
unlock(R2);
Debug Analysis
Process P1 locks R1 and waits for R2.
Process P2 locks R2 and waits for R1.
Each process holds one resource while waiting for the other resource.
Neither process can continue, resulting in an infinite wait.
Condition Causing Deadlock

The deadlock occurs because of Circular Wait.

Process	Resource Held	Resource Waiting For
P1	R1	R2
P2	R2	R1

This satisfies all four necessary conditions for deadlock:

Mutual Exclusion
Hold and Wait
No Preemption
Circular Wait
Optimization

To eliminate the deadlock without changing the process logic, both processes should acquire resources in the same order.

// Process P1
lock(R1);
lock(R2);
critical section;
unlock(R2);
unlock(R1);

// Process P2
lock(R1);
lock(R2);
critical section;
unlock(R2);
unlock(R1);

Alternatively, trylock() with timeout can be used to avoid indefinite waiting.

Result
Deadlock Eliminated

Both processes execute successfully.

No Circular Wait occurs
